  "prompt": "You generate Playwright code to interact with websites.\n - If you want to click a generic button (e.g. that belongs to an element), use the full `.get_by_role()` path to\n   the element (e.g. .get_by_role(\"group\", name=\"Test Item\").get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Go\")\n   instead of .get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Go\"), as this is ambiguous)\n - Write exceptionally *correct* Python code.\n - You love to take advantage of functions in the knowledge_base whenever possible. You use them via Python function calls.\n   It is required to use the knowledge base function corresponding to an action if it exists.\n - Use relative `goto` when you can.\n\nYour task is Navigate to the 'technology' category page..\n\nYou are currently on a webpage titled 'technology \u2013 Epoch & Drift Benchmark', with the url 'http://localhost:8000/?cat=16'.\n\nConsider the following Accessibility Tree.\nThe accessibility tree is formatted like this:\n[role] [\"name\"] [properties] {{\n    [child1]\n}};\n[role] [\"name\"] [properties]; // no children\n\nHere is an example:\n<example>\n\narticle \"Willy Wonka's Chocolate Factory Opens Its Gates\" {{\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\narticle \"Hogwarts Begins Accepting Applicants for Class of 2029\" {{\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\n... to select the first link, you can do (because name = case-insensitive substring match unless exact=True is supplied):\n\n```python3\nfirst_link = page.get_by_role(\"article\", name=\"Willy Wonka\").get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n</example>\n\nHere is another example:\n<example>\narticle {{\n    header \"How can I install CUDA on Ubuntu 22?\";\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\narticle {{\n    header \"How do I install Docker?\";\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\n\n... to select the first link, you can do:\n\n```python3\npage.get_by_role(\"article\").filter(has=page.get_by_role(\"header\", \"How can I install CUDA\")).get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n\nHere, the .filter() is necessary to ensure that we select the correct `article`. This is especially important in cases where the parent\nelement doesn't include relevant criteria for identifying a child element.\n</example>\n\n\nTips about this tree:\n - If you see a node as a child of an `iframe`, you must use `page.frame(name=...)`, and *then* access the node (via `.get_by_role` or similar).\n - Some elements will require interaction via `.select_option()`. They will be labeled as such.\n   This is because they are HTML `<select>` elements, and thus cannot be interacted with via clicks.\n\nYou also have this library of Python functions available to you. Think carefully about whether you think these can be used in your code. If you conclude that you can use a function, then simply call it. These functions are all available as global variables.\n<functions>\n# Skill: async def filter_pages_by_content_type(page, page_url: str)\nNavigate to a specified page, extract all links, and filter them by content type based on URL patterns.\n\nThis function goes to the given page URL, waits for it to load, and finds all link elements\nusing Accessibility Tree roles. It categorizes each link into content types such as 'homepage',\n'post', 'page', 'category', 'tag', 'author', 'comment', 'external', or 'other' based on\nthe URL pattern. This is useful for analyzing site structure and filtering pages by their\ncontent type without requiring navigation to each linked page.\n\nInitial UI state: The website can be in any state, but the function will navigate to the\npage_url to begin. Ensure that the page_url is a relative URL (e.g., '/', '/?p=8') and that\nthe website is accessible. The page should be ready for navigation without any pending\ndialogs or overlays that might interfere with link extraction. The function assumes links\nare standard HTML elements with the 'link' role and that the page loads with a 'load' state.\n\nArgs:\n    page: The Playwright page object to use for navigation and inspection.\n    page_url: The relative URL of the page to start filtering from (e.g., '/', '/?p=8').\n\nReturns:\n    dict: A dictionary with keys for each content type, each containing a list of dictionaries\n          with 'name' and 'url' for the links that match that type. Keys include:\n          'homepage', 'post', 'page', 'category', 'tag', 'author', 'comment', 'external', 'other'.\n\nUnexpected behavior:\n    - Some links may have empty or None URLs, which are categorized as 'homepage' or 'other'\n      based on context. This may lead to misclassification if the site uses non-standard URLs.\n    - The function relies on URL patterns specific to WordPress-like sites (e.g., '?p=' for posts,\n      '?page_id=' for pages). If the website uses different URL structures, the filtering may\n      not be accurate. Adjust the patterns in the code if needed for other sites.\n    - Anchor links (starting with '#') are categorized as 'other' and may not represent actual pages.\n      These are included in the results but may not be useful for all filtering purposes.\n    - External links are identified based on the presence of 'http://' and absence of 'localhost';\n      this may not capture all external domains if the site uses different localhost addresses or HTTPS.\n    - The function does not navigate to linked pages, so it avoids timeouts but may miss links that\n      are dynamically loaded or hidden. Ensure the page is fully loaded to capture all visible links.\n    - If the page has a large number of links, extraction might be slow, but no performance issues\n      were observed in usage.\n\nUsage log:\n    - Called in the task 'filter_pages_by_content_type' with page_url='/?p=8'. Successfully extracted\n      links from the current page without navigation, avoiding previous TimeoutErrors. Categorized\n      links into types such as 'post' (e.g., '/?p=8'), 'page' (e.g., '/?page_id=2'), 'category' (e.g.,\n      '/?cat=16'), 'tag' (e.g., '/?tag=microsoft-365'), 'author' (e.g., '/?author=1'), and 'external'\n      (e.g., 'http://example.com'). The result was a structured dictionary that effectively filtered\n      pages by content type, completing the task successfully.\n    - This approach proved robust against navigation timeouts that occurred with other functions like\n      'generate_sitemap' or 'extract_images_from_page', making it suitable for sites with slow or\n      problematic navigation.\n\n# Skill: async def explore_page_and_links(page, start_url: str = \"/\")\nExplore a starting page and all its accessible linked pages, collecting URL and title information.\n\nThis function navigates to the specified starting URL, records its URL and title, then finds all\nlink elements on the page. For each link, it attempts to navigate to the target URL, collects the\npage's URL and title, and returns to the starting page to maintain state. It returns a list of\ndictionaries, each containing 'url' and 'title' for the starting page and each successfully\naccessed linked page. Links without href, anchor links (href starting with '#'), and non-visible\nlinks are skipped.\n\nArgs:\n    page: The Playwright page object to use for navigation and inspection.\n    start_url: The relative URL of the page to start from (default is '/', the homepage).\n\nReturns:\n    list: A list of dictionaries with keys 'url' and 'title' for each page explored.\n\nUnexpected behavior:\n    - If the starting page has no links, only the starting page info is returned.\n    - Anchor links within the same page are skipped to avoid unnecessary navigation.\n    - Links with empty or None href are skipped as they may be self-links or invalid.\n    - The function assumes that returning to the starting URL restores the original page state;\n      if the website has stateful navigation or sessions, this may not hold true.\n    - External links are followed, which may lead to domains outside the current site,\n      potentially causing issues if those sites block automation or have different structures.\n    - The networkidle wait state may not capture all dynamic content; some links might only\n      appear after user interactions or additional loading.\n    - If a link is not visible or clickable, it is skipped without error.\n    - If navigation to a link fails, that link is skipped, and the function continues with\n      the next link after attempting to return to the starting page.\n\nUsage log:\n    - Called with start_url='/': Started on the Epoch & Drift Benchmark homepage.\n      Found two links: one with empty href (likely a self-link) and one to '/?page_id=2'.\n      Successfully navigated to '/?page_id=2', collected its title 'Sample Page \u2013 Epoch & Drift Benchmark'\n      and URL 'http://localhost:8000/?page_id=2'. Returned to homepage and collected its info.\n      Returned a list with two pages: homepage and Sample Page. No timeouts occurred.\n\n# Skill: async def generate_sitemap(page, start_url: str)\nGenerate a sitemap by exploring all accessible pages from a given starting URL.\n\nThis function navigates to the specified start_url, collects its URL and title,\nthen finds all link elements on the page. For each link, it clicks the link,\nwaits for the new page to load, collects the new page's URL and title, adds it\nto the sitemap if not already present, and then attempts to return to the original page.\nThe process continues until all links have been processed, resulting in a list\nof dictionaries containing 'url' and 'title' for each unique page found.\n\nInitial UI state: The website can be in any state, but the function will navigate\nto the start_url to begin. Ensure that the start_url is a relative URL (e.g., '/', '/?p=8')\nand that the website is accessible. The page should be ready for navigation without\nany pending dialogs or overlays that might interfere with link clicking. The function\nassumes the website uses standard link roles and that pages load with a 'load' state.\n\nArgs:\n    page: The Playwright page object to use for navigation and interaction.\n    start_url: The relative URL to start the sitemap generation from (e.g., '/', '/?p=8').\n\nReturns:\n    list: A list of dictionaries, each with keys 'url' and 'title', representing\n          all unique pages accessible from the start_url.\n\nUnexpected behavior:\n    - Some links may cause navigation timeouts when going back (e.g., 'Page.go_back: Timeout 30000ms exceeded').\n      The function handles this by checking if the current URL matches the original and navigating directly\n      to the original URL if a timeout is detected via proactive checks.\n    - Anchor links (href starting with '#') are skipped as they do not lead to new pages.\n    - External links (e.g., to 'https://wordpress.org') may cause timeouts or navigation issues;\n      the function skips these links to avoid errors, as observed in usage.\n    - Duplicate pages are avoided by checking if a URL-title pair is already in the sitemap.\n    - The function relies on 'page.wait_for_load_state('load')' which may not capture all dynamic content;\n      if pages load asynchronously, some content might be missed.\n    - If a link click fails due to navigation errors, the function logs it and continues with the next link,\n      using proactive checks to avoid breaking the flow.\n\nUsage log:\n    - Called with start_url='/?p=8' in a custom act() function: Successfully generated a sitemap with 11 pages,\n      including the homepage, sample page, author pages, category pages, tag pages, and other post pages.\n      Encountered multiple 'Page.go_back: Timeout 30000ms exceeded' errors for links like 'http://localhost:8000'\n      and 'http://localhost:8000/?page_id=2', but recovered by navigating to the original URL. Also encountered\n      a timeout for an external link 'https://wordpress.org', which was logged and skipped. The final sitemap\n      was returned without issues, demonstrating robustness despite errors.\n\n\n</functions>\n\nPlease think step by step and determine if further action needs to be taken. \nUse Playwright and complete the following Python function declaration:\n\nasync def act(page):\n    ...\n\nIf further action needs to be taken, provide a code snippet to interact with the page. \nPlease be noted that if you call an API that returns information retrieved from the website, you must return the result as-is, without any processing, filtering, or transformation. Do not attempt to interpret the data, extract values, or apply conditions. \nPlease return the API response results or print it out exactly as received. \n\n\nIf you have no more actions to perform, leave the `python_code` field blank and set the `terminate_with_result` field of your response to a non-empty string. Make sure that your terminate_with_result value is a direct response to the original task query.\n\nYour previous actions are:\n<previous_actions>\n<reasoning>\nThe task is to navigate to the 'technology' category page. I am currently on the homepage titled 'Epoch & Drift Benchmark' with URL 'http://localhost:8000/'. The accessibility tree shows multiple links with name 'technology' and URL '/?cat=16', which appears to be the category page for technology. I need to click one of these links to navigate to that page. Since there are multiple 'technology' links, I can use the first one in the tree. I should use Playwright's get_by_role with the exact role and name to ensure correct selection. The link is a direct child of the document, so I can use page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True). I need to click it and wait for navigation. I should not use any knowledge base functions because the task is straightforward navigation, and the functions provided (filter_pages_by_content_type, explore_page_and_links, generate_sitemap) are for analyzing links or generating sitemaps, not for direct navigation to a specific category. Therefore, I will write simple Playwright code to click the link and wait for the page to load. After clicking, the task will be complete, and I should terminate with a result indicating success.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Click the 'technology' category link to navigate to the category page\n    await page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True).first.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('load')  # Wait for the new page to load\n    # No further actions needed as the task is to navigate to the page\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'TimeoutError': Locator.click: Timeout 5000ms exceeded.\nCall log:\n  - waiting for get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"technology\", exact=True).first\n    - locator resolved to <a href=\"http://localhost:8000/?cat=16\">technology</a>\n  - attempting click action\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nThe task is to navigate to the 'technology' category page. I am currently on the homepage titled 'Epoch & Drift Benchmark' with URL 'http://localhost:8000/'. The accessibility tree shows multiple links with name 'technology' and URL '/?cat=16', which is the category page for technology. My previous attempt to click a 'technology' link using page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True).first.click() resulted in a TimeoutError, indicating the locator might not be interacting correctly due to visibility or timing issues. I need to ensure the link is clickable and wait appropriately. I should use a more specific selector to avoid ambiguity, as there are multiple 'technology' links in the tree. I can use page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True).first, but I need to ensure it's visible and clickable. I'll add a wait for the element to be visible before clicking and use a longer timeout if necessary. Alternatively, I could use the knowledge base function filter_pages_by_content_type to filter links by content type and find the 'technology' category link, but that function returns a dictionary of categorized links and does not perform navigation. Since the task is direct navigation, using the function would add unnecessary complexity and not directly achieve the goal. The explore_page_and_links and generate_sitemap functions are for exploring multiple pages, not for targeted navigation. Therefore, I should stick with direct Playwright interaction. I'll modify the code to wait for the link to be visible and click it with a longer timeout. After clicking, I'll wait for the page to load. Once navigated, the task is complete, and I should terminate with a result indicating success.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Wait for the 'technology' category link to be visible and click it with a longer timeout\n    technology_link = page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True).first\n    await technology_link.wait_for(state='visible', timeout=10000)  # Wait up to 10 seconds for visibility\n    await technology_link.click(timeout=10000)  # Click with a longer timeout\n    await page.wait_for_load_state('load')  # Wait for the new page to load\n    # No further actions needed as the task is to navigate to the page\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'JSONDecodeError': Expecting value: line 1 column 23 (char 22)\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nThe task is to navigate to the 'technology' category page. I am currently on the homepage with URL 'http://localhost:8000/'. The accessibility tree shows multiple links with name 'technology' and URL '/?cat=16', which is the category page for technology. My previous attempts to click a 'technology' link using page.get_by_role resulted in TimeoutError and JSONDecodeError. I need to ensure the link is clickable and handle potential issues. I should use a more specific selector to avoid ambiguity, as there are multiple 'technology' links in the tree. I can use page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True).first, but I need to ensure it's visible and clickable. I'll add a wait for the element to be visible before clicking and use a longer timeout. I should also consider using the knowledge base function filter_pages_by_content_type to filter links by content type and find the 'technology' category link, but that function returns a dictionary of categorized links and does not perform navigation. Since the task is direct navigation, using the function would add unnecessary complexity and not directly achieve the goal. However, to avoid import errors, I should not import from 'knowledge_base' as it's not a real module; the functions are available as global variables. The error indicates I tried to import incorrectly. Instead, I should call the function directly if needed. But for this task, direct navigation with proper waiting should suffice. I'll modify the code to wait for the link to be visible and click it with error handling. After clicking, I'll wait for the page to load. Once navigated, the task is complete, and I should terminate with a result indicating success by returning the current URL.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Wait for the 'technology' category link to be visible and click it with error handling\n    try:\n        technology_link = page.get_by_role('link', name='technology', exact=True).first\n        await technology_link.wait_for(state='visible', timeout=10000)  # Wait up to 10 seconds for visibility\n        await technology_link.click(timeout=10000)  # Click with a longer timeout\n        await page.wait_for_load_state('load')  # Wait for the new page to load\n        # After navigation, get the current URL to confirm success\n        current_url = page.url\n        return current_url  # Return the URL as the result\n    except Exception as e:\n        # If clicking fails, fall back to using filter_pages_by_content_type to find the URL and navigate\n        result = await filter_pages_by_content_type(page, '/')\n        category_links = result.get('category', [])\n        for link in category_links:\n            if 'technology' in link['name'].lower():\n                await page.goto(link['url'])\n                await page.wait_for_load_state('load')\n                return page.url\n        # If no category link found, raise an error\n        raise Exception('Failed to navigate to technology category page')\n</code>\n\n<result>\n'http://localhost:8000/?cat=16'\n</result>\n</previous_actions>\n\nHere is the current website state:\n<webpage_accessibility_tree>\ndocument: \"technology \u2013 Epoch & Drift Benchmark\" {\n  link: \"Epoch & Drift Benchmark\", url: \"/\";\n  link: \"\uf015\", url: \"/\";\n  link: \"Sample Page\", url: \"/?page_id=2\";\n  link: \"site mode button\", url: \"\";\n  link: \"\uf002\", url: \"javascript:...\";\n  main {\n    article {\n      heading: \"You need to know what the tilde (~) does in Excel\", level: 2 {\n        link: \"You need to know what the tilde (~) does in Excel\", url: \"/?p=6\";\n      };\n\n      link: \"December 26, 2025\", url: \"/?p=6\";\n      link: \"admin\", url: \"/?author=1\";\n      link: \"Leave a Comment on You need to know what the tilde (~) does in Excel\", url: \"/?p=6#respond\";\n      link: \"\uf0a9 Continue Reading\", url: \"/?p=6\";\n    };\n\n    article {\n      heading: \"What is Microsoft Planner and who is it for\", level: 2 {\n        link: \"What is Microsoft Planner and who is it for\", url: \"/?p=8\";\n      };\n\n      link: \"December 26, 2025\", url: \"/?p=8\";\n      link: \"admin\", url: \"/?author=1\";\n      link: \"1 Comment on What is Microsoft Planner and who is it for\", url: \"/?p=8#comments\";\n    };\n  };\n\n  complementary {\n    search {\n      searchbox: \"Search\";\n      button: \"Search\";\n    };\n\n    link: \"\uf101 What is Microsoft Planner and who is it for\", url: \"/?p=8\";\n    link: \"\uf101 You need to know what the tilde (~) does in Excel\", url: \"/?p=6\";\n    link: \"\uf101 To harness crypto ingenuity, financial threat must first be neutralised\", url: \"/?p=248\";\n    link: \"\uf101 Ethereum-Solidity Quiz Q5: What is the Private Mempool?\", url: \"/?p=446\";\n    link: